\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Real-life example: Customer support refund agent\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 An e-commerce company has an AI customer-support agent. A customer says:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \'93My order was cancelled. Please refund \uc0\u8377 2,000.\'94\
The agent calls the company\'92s payment API:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 POST /refund\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 The payment system processes the refund, but the API response times out.\
Now the AI agent does not know whether the refund happened.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Without ToolSwap\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The agent retries the refund request.\
The customer may receive two refunds, costing the company \uc0\u8377 4,000.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 With ToolSwap\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The company places ToolSwap between the AI agent and its APIs.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 Customer request\
      \uc0\u8595 \
AI support agent\
      \uc0\u8595 \
Payment API\
      \uc0\u8595 \
ToolSwap recovery system\
      \uc0\u8595 \
Safe next action\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 When the timeout occurs:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 ToolSwap receives the failed API response.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 It checks the tool contract and sees that refunds create financial side effects.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The recovery LLM recommends:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 \{\
  "action": "VERIFY",\
  "tool": "payments.get_refund_status",\
  "reason": "The refund may have completed before the timeout"\
\}\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The safety controller blocks another refund attempt.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 ToolSwap calls the refund-status API using the transaction or idempotency key.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The status API confirms that the refund already succeeded.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	7	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The agent tells the customer:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \'93Your \uc0\u8377 2,000 refund has been processed.\'94\
No duplicate refund is created.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 How a company integrates it\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The company connects ToolSwap to its agent as recovery middleware:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 Agent calls tool\
      \uc0\u8595 \
Tool succeeds \uc0\u8594  continue normally\
      \uc0\u8595 \
Tool fails or result is uncertain\
      \uc0\u8595 \
Send task, tool contract, error and history to ToolSwap\
      \uc0\u8595 \
ToolSwap recommends VERIFY, RETRY, SWITCH or STOP\
      \uc0\u8595 \
Safety controller approves or blocks the action\
      \uc0\u8595 \
Agent continues safely\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 ToolSwap does not replace the company\'92s AI agent or APIs. It adds a reliability layer around them.\
The practical benefit is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Companies can use ToolSwap to prevent AI agents from blindly retrying failed operations, causing duplicate transactions, using unsafe fallback tools, or claiming that a task succeeded without proof.\
